Chapter 34: The Baseline Metric
The sirens above Ring 3 shifted from a steady hum to a sharp, double-pulsing red rhythm.
BEE-BEEP. BEE-BEEP.
"Wave Three initiating," the automated arena system announced over the speakers. "Threat Matrix escalated to Tier-C Peak. Environmental hazard engaged."
The impact floor beneath Squad 4 shuddered. Heavy vents along the perimeter ring hissed violently, releasing a dense, low-hanging cloud of synthetic obscuring fog. Within seconds, visibility inside the barrier dropped to less than three feet.
"Fog deployment?" Tobias coughed, raising his arm to block the acrid vapor. "They didn't use fog for the other squads!"
"Keep tight!" Renna yelled, stepping back until her heavy tower shield pressed firmly against Tobias and Mina’s shoulders. "Mina, use your ears! Tobias, don't fire until you have a solid target profile!"
From the high observation deck, Evaluator Vane glanced at Captain Drake, whose hand was resting directly on the arena control console.
"You manual-overrode the environmental parameters, Drake," Vane said, his voice cold and analytical. "That squad is an auxiliary reserve unit. Pushing a Tier-C Peak construct with zero-visibility parameters exceeds standard baseline protocol."
"Something about their kinetic dispersion is wrong," Drake replied, his scarred face illuminated by the green glow of the tactical monitors. "Two collisions, perfect shield dampening, and zero casualties. Either their shield-bearer possesses an unmapped passive trait... or someone inside that ring is manipulating the vectors."
Drake tapped the screen, authorizing the release. "Let's see them maneuver when they can't see the incoming strike."
Down inside the foggy ring, heavy, rhythmic thuds echoed through the metal floor.
THUD. THUD. THUD.
Out of the purple-tinged vapor emerged a Siege-Breaker Sentinel (Tier-C Peak). Standing nine feet tall, the massive automaton was covered in interlocking composite armor, its right arm equipped with a pneumatic impact ram capable of breaching reinforced bunker gates.
"It's huge!" Tobias yelled, trying to aim his crossbow into the mist, but the fog distorted the machine's thermal core.
The Sentinel didn't stall. It raised its pneumatic ram, the internal pressure chamber roaring like a jet turbine, and lunged straight through the fog at Renna’s center mass.
WHIRRR-BOOM!
"Brace!" Renna screamed, locking both feet into the floor and channeling every ounce of her aetheric energy into her shield.
Kaelen slouched on one knee directly behind Renna's left heel, his hands clutching a spare leather strap.
To anyone watching through the distorted thermal cameras, he was just a terrified caddy cowering in the mist. But behind his lowered hood, Kaelen’s [Low-Light Spatial Perception] tore right through the synthetic fog, rendering the nine-foot Sentinel in crystal-clear grayscale.
His [Hive-Mind Synapse] mapped the impact vector instantly.
The pneumatic ram is cycling at 4,000 RPM, Kaelen calculated. Renna's shield wall will hold the initial point, but the secondary hydraulic recoil will snap her left collarbone and shatter the barrier floor.
Kaelen didn't hesitate. He didn't use an flashy skill or reveal his frame.
As the Sentinel’s massive ram slammed into the face of Renna’s tower shield, Kaelen subtly shifted his weight forward, pressing his right boot firmly against the rear anchor-plate of Renna’s shield base.
He engaged [Terrestrial Anchor] and activated his custom Anchor-Greaves.
ZZZZT.
A micro-pulse of pure, unyielding kinetic stability transferred through Kaelen’s boot straight into the shield’s foundation.
BOOOOOOM!
The concussive shockwave blew the surrounding fog fifty feet back, revealing the entire ring to the stadium.
The nine-foot Sentinel’s pneumatic ram struck with five tons of force—and stopped dead.
Renna didn't slide back an inch. The floor beneath her boots didn't even crack. Instead, the monstrous kinetic energy of the ram had nowhere to go; the entire force bounced straight back into the Sentinel’s internal piston mechanism.
CRACK-THUD!
The Sentinel’s pneumatic arm burst open from internal pressure, sending gears and hydraulic fluid blasting out of its shoulder joint. The massive machine staggered backward, completely unbalanced and defenseless.
"NOW!" Renna ordered, her voice ringing with absolute authority.
Tobias didn't miss. He fired his scatter-bolt straight into the exposed, ruptured shoulder seam. Mina lunged forward, sliding between the Sentinel's massive legs and severing the main power conduit behind its left knee.
EXPLOSION!
The nine-foot Tier-C Peak Sentinel collapsed to the arena floor, its glowing red eye fading to black as its mana core shut down.
BZZZZZT!
The blue energy barrier dissolved. The fog vents shut off.
[PERFORMANCE BASELINE AUDIT COMPLETE]
[Ring 3: Anvil Ridge Squad 4]
[Status: CLEAR (Zero Casualties / Zero Barrier Violations)]
Complete silence fell over Sub-Level 1. The surrounding squads of corporate freshmen stared in open-mouthed shock at the smoking wreckage of the Tier-C Peak Sentinel.
Up on the observation balcony, Captain Drake leaned close to his monitor, his eyes fixed on the precise millisecond of impact.
He replayed the footage three times. The camera showed Renna holding the shield, while Kaelen was crouched behind her, seemingly holding his head with one boot braced against the metal base.
"Her shield anchor rating hit 98.4%," Evaluator Vane said, tapping his tablet with a slight, impressed nod. "That is higher than the baseline of our Tier-B Senior Vanguards. Renna Vance’s defensive talent is far higher than Anvil Ridge’s initial reports suggested."
Drake stared at the screen for a long moment, his broad chest rising and falling. He still felt a lingering thread of suspicion, but the biometric data on Kaelen showed zero active mana signatures—only a terrified, low-tier auxiliary holding a leather strap.
"Final score logged," Drake grunted, pressing the authorization button.
Down in the arena, the central digital scoreboard flashed with golden text.
VANGUARD FRESHMAN DIVISION — BASELINE AUDIT RESULTS
 * Ironclad Squad 1 — Score: 94/100 (Tier-A Standard)
 * Anvil Ridge Squad 4 — Score: 92/100 (Class-A Reserve Verified)
 * Gideon Squad 1 — Score: 88/100 (Tier-B Standard)
Tobias let out a deafening yell, grabbing Mina by the shoulders and swinging her around. "NINETY-TWO! WE GOT A NINETY-TWO!"
Mina laughed hysterically, wiping oil from her cheek. "We beat Gideon’s primary team!"
Renna dropped her shield to the floor, breathing heavily, tears of massive relief shining in her amber eyes. She turned to her squad, then looked down at Kaelen, who was sitting on the floor, wiping his dirty forehead with his sleeve while offering a timid, happy grin.
"We passed our first audit," Renna whispered, kneeling down and pulling Kaelen into a sudden, tight hug. "We secured our reserve funding for the next three months!"
"G-Great job, Renna," Kaelen stammered meekly, patting her back with an awkward, hesitant hand. "Your shield was amazing!"
"We did it together," Renna said firmly, pulling back with a fierce smile.
As the squad celebrated and gathered their gear to head to the equipment bays, Kaelen fell three paces behind them, picking up his tool roll and slipping his dirty rag back into his belt.
He glanced subtly toward the high observation balcony, catching Captain Drake’s sharp eyes before the instructor turned away.
Underneath his loose utility vest, Kaelen’s Level 14 core settled back into its quiet, unyielding rhythm.
The baseline score was locked. Renna’s squad was safe from corporate audits for the next ninety days, and Kaelen’s position as a "harmless utility caddy" was officially cemented into the Guild's records.
He reached into his pocket, feeling the cold metal of his Class-B Caddy Pass.
The daytime testing was over. Tonight, Sub-Dungeon 4 would be open again—and the Void-Scale Hydra was only the first course.
